  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A cold front will cross the region today, producing \nseveral rounds of showers and scattered thunderstorms with uneven \ncoverage.\n\nA broad mid/upper level trough will remain in place across eastern \nCanada and the adjacent US portion of the Great Lakes today. A mid \nlevel shortwave and associated pre-frontal surface trough will cross \nWestern NY this morning before reaching the eastern Lake Ontario \nregion midday into the afternoon. Large scale ascent and moisture \nconvergence associated with these features will provide focus for an \narea of showers and a few scattered thunderstorms crossing Western \nNY through early to mid morning before reaching the eastern Lake \nOntario region midday into the afternoon.\n\nCoverage of thunder will be sparse this morning across Western NY \ngiven the lack of instability. Expect coverage and intensity of \nthunder to increase midday through the afternoon east of Lake \nOntario as the pre-frontal trough encounters increasing diurnal \ninstability. Surface temperatures in the lower 80s and dewpoints in \nthe mid to upper 60s will contribute to MLCAPE of around 1000J/kg \nthis afternoon across Central NY, although forecast soundings \nsuggest a rather tall/skinny CAPE profile. Modest to borderline \nmoderate shear profiles will support the potential for a few \nmulticell clusters and short bowing segments across the eastern Lake \nOntario region from midday into the afternoon with a low end risk of \na few severe thunderstorms. The main risk will be isolated strong \nwind gusts and brief heavy downpours. \n\nA few more scattered showers and thunderstorms will develop this \nafternoon behind the pre-frontal trough across Western NY along lake \nbreeze boundaries, mainly from the Southern Tier into the Finger \nLakes and in a narrow strip from the Niagara River to Orleans \nCounty. Meanwhile, increasing southwest flow will allow a stable \nlake shadow to expand from Buffalo ENE to Rochester this afternoon, \nreducing the chance of additional convection along that corridor.\n\nAny remaining scattered showers and thunderstorms will quickly end \nearly this evening as the boundary layer stabilizes and forcing \nmoves east of the area. \n\nKey Message 2...A weak secondary cold front may produce a few \nscattered light showers overnight through Friday morning.\n\nA weak secondary cold front will move southeast across the eastern \nGreat Lakes overnight through early Friday morning. Gradually \ncooling temperatures aloft will allow some modest lake enhancement \nto develop briefly along and just behind the cold front, which may \nsupport a few scattered light showers southeast of Lake Erie and \nLake Ontario overnight through Friday morning. Post-frontal low \nlevel moisture will support widespread stratus Friday morning before \ngradual clearing from northwest to southeast Friday afternoon.\n\nKey Message 3...Dry weather will return Friday afternoon and last \nthrough the weekend.\n\nSurface high pressure will build east across the Great Lakes Friday \nafternoon through Saturday before drifting off the New England coast \nSunday. This feature will support a dry and very pleasant final \nweekend of August. Slightly below average temperatures Friday \nthrough Friday night will give way to a warming trend over the \nweekend, with above average temperatures arriving by Sunday.\n\nA tightening thermal gradient and moisture convergence may support a \nfew showers and thunderstorms Sunday afternoon from the upper Great \nLakes to eastern Ontario, but most model and ensemble guidance \nsuggests this rain will stay mainly north of our region until Sunday \nnight or Monday.",
